{1 Blocking Gaps}

The following gaps must be closed before the paper can honestly claim that
the implementation follows the Rocq-backed mathematical formalization.

The Stage 1 gaps must be handled by the case-by-case arbitration recorded in
{!page-stage1_ocaml_rocq_arbitration}. That page decides, for each mismatch,
whether Rocq, OCaml, presentation, or only a derived/backend view should
change.

{2 B1. Surface-generated checks}

OCaml transitions carry [ensures], but the current parser does not expose a
general syntax for arbitrary transition-local postconditions. Ordinary
transition productions initialize [ensures] to [[]]. The proof-relevant
[ensures] observed in the pipeline are generated mainly by history
elaboration: [history init ensures] and [history step ensures] are attached
to each expanded transition after ghost history updates.

These generated checks are not diagnostics and not backend noise: they are
compiled as Why3 postconditions. They are, however, surface-elaboration
checks, not Rocq [pssEnsures].

The clean rule is:

- do not add history definitions, observers, or other syntactic sugar to the
  Rocq formalization;
- take the elaborated core program as the Rocq-facing program;
- keep sugar-generated proof checks outside the canonical Rocq summary
  [ensures], or classify them in a separate elaboration-check family.

The implementation now exposes this boundary explicitly:
[program_step.elaboration_checks] flows into
[product_step_summary.elaboration_checks]. The canonical
[product_step_summary.ensures] field is reserved for structural product
ensures corresponding to Rocq [pssEnsures].

{2 G2. Initial invariant goals}

Rocq currently has [kairosInitInvariantGoals ps = []]. OCaml generates
[init_invariant_goals] as the empty list.

The mathematical rule is the Rocq one: an initial state is not a state in
which an ordinary state invariant has to be re-established by an incoming
step. It is the starting point of runs.

OCaml now aligns with this rule. Surface state-invariant expansion rejects any
selector whose final denotation contains the initial state, the verification
model validator rejects initial-state invariants, and [Pre.run_node] no longer
creates initial invariant goals. Examples that used [in Init] as an
initialization reminder were migrated to explicit initialization code instead
of being silently kept as ordinary state invariants.

{2 G3. Candidate generation}

Rocq Stage 1 enumerates all real indexed source transitions plus one implicit
default-skip transition per source state, against all assumption and guarantee
automaton transitions, then filters for relevance. The operational meaning of
[Default] is: no explicit outgoing transition from the current source state is
enabled, so the program skips and stays in the same control state.

OCaml now materializes the same source semantics in
[Verification_model.normalize_node_semantics]: after source-order
prioritization, it appends one generated self-loop step per control state with
an empty body and a guard expressing that no original outgoing guard from that
state is true. Generated defaults whose guard is syntactically [false] are
omitted, because the fallback is then unreachable by construction. This keeps
explicit transition indices stable and makes reachable implicit skips visible
to product exploration and proof obligation generation.

OCaml builds product data by exploration from the initial product state. That
can be the right implementation, but the adequacy statement must say why it
generates exactly the product candidates needed by the Rocq proof, or else
the two sides must be aligned.

{2 G4. Kernel clause families}

The principle to compare with Rocq is the generated family of source and
product clauses. [Kernel_clause_projection] is only a derived OCaml view that
may expose this family to proof export or diagnostics; it is not the formal
definition of Stage 1.

The current view is close to the Rocq vocabulary ([KernelClause],
[TimedFact], product anchors), but the family contents do not currently match
Rocq Stage 1 exactly:

- Rocq has entry clauses for each summary requirement;
- Rocq phase clauses range over [pssEnsures];
- OCaml phase clauses currently expose guarantee guards and compatibility
  formulas.

This must be resolved before claiming that the implementation exposes the
same Stage 1 clauses as the Rocq principle.

{2 G5. Step-contract granularity}

Rocq Stage 2 gives one bad-guarantee contract per unsafe case. OCaml now
exposes that granularity in [Canonical_obligations.build_stage2].

[Step_contract_projection] is a derived grouped view of the canonical family:
safe contracts are kept as canonical summary contracts; unsafe contracts with
the same summary identity are grouped for the backend, and top-level
disjunctions may be split into several forbidden formulas. This grouping is
outside the Rocq principle. Its required preservation argument is:

{[
For each grouped unsafe contract G and every canonical unsafe contract C
covered by G, proving all forbidden formulas emitted by G under the shared
pre/exec relation proves C's forbidden formula.
]}

The implementation keeps the link explicit through [covered_cases] and the
[canonical] field carried by [Step_contract_projection.t].

{1 Clean Adequacy Target}

The implementation should expose a canonical proof boundary with these
mathematical relations:

{[
CoreProgramAdequate(ocaml_model, rocq_ps)

SuppliedAutomataAdequate(ocaml_automata, rocq_ca)

Stage1Adequate:
  OCaml product summaries and kernel clause families
  are equivalent to kairosSummariesFromSourceAndContracts and
  kairosSummaryFamilies.

Stage2Adequate:
  OCaml canonical step contracts are equivalent to
  kairosStage2StepContracts, before backend grouping.

BackendPreservesValidity:
  If the backend discharges its grouped VCs, then
  GeneratedObligationsValid holds.
]}

Only [Stage1Adequate] and [Stage2Adequate] are about the proof-generation
principle. [BackendPreservesValidity] is about Why3 encoding, not about the
Rocq mathematical kernel.

{1 Consequence For The Paper}

The paper should present the Rocq-backed mathematical principle first. The
implementation section should then state:

- which implementation objects are the witnesses for the mathematical
  objects;
- which transformations are merely obligation-preserving optimizations;
- which components are outside the theorem.

At the current point, the paper must not claim that the existing OCaml
pipeline is fully matched by the Rocq development. The correct claim is that
Rocq gives the intended proof principle, and this audit identifies the exact
implementation/formalization adequacy obligations that remain.
